          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "g(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Find the domain of the following.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p141-q21-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(g(x))$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-\\infty,0)\\cup(0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=f\\left(\\frac{1}{x-1}\\right)\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x-1}+1}\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{x-1}{x}\\end{align*}$$\nThe simplified rule requires $x\\ne0$. The inner function $g$ also requires $x\\ne1$; this restriction remains after simplification.\nBoth restrictions must hold, so the domain is\n$$(-\\infty,0)\\cup(0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-q21-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$g(f(x))$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-\\infty,-1)\\cup(-1,0)\\cup(0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}g(f(x))&=g\\left(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\right)\\\\g(f(x))&=\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x+1}-1}\\\\g(f(x))&=-\\frac{x+1}{x}\\end{align*}$$\nThe simplified rule requires $x\\ne0$. The inner function $f$ also requires $x\\ne-1$; this restriction remains after simplification.\nBoth restrictions must hold, so the domain is\n$$(-\\infty,-1)\\cup(-1,0)\\cup(0,\\infty).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      }
    ]
  },
